course: dictionary
lesson: 2449
title: Dandelion seeds on the move
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A dandelion is a common wild plant with a bright yellow flower. When the flower goes over, it turns into a white, fluffy ball of seeds, which is called a clock. Blowing on the clock sends the seeds off into the air.
explain: «Seeds must travel away from their parent» so that young plants do not compete with it for light and water. Each dandelion seed has a tiny parachute, so the wind can carry it far away.
explain: Look at the word «clock». It is not a clock for telling the time, but children still blow on it to tell the time or to make a wish. The name comes from the round, fluffy shape of the seed ball.
fact: A dandelion is a common wild plant with a bright yellow flower.
fact: The white, fluffy ball of seeds on a dandelion is called a clock.
fact: «Dandelion seeds float on tiny parachutes», so the wind can carry them away.
fact: The name dandelion comes from French words meaning «lion's tooth», because the leaves have jagged teeth.
fact: Dandelion flowers close at night and in rain.
exercise: What is a dandelion? | A kind of bird that eats seeds | *A common wild plant with a bright yellow flower | A stone with a shiny surface | A tree that grows in deserts
exercise: «Some go by air: blow a dandelion clock and each seed floats off on a tiny parachute.» What helps the seeds travel? | Heavy stones | Sharp teeth | *Tiny parachutes | Deep roots
exercise: The name dandelion comes from French words meaning what? | Yellow flower | *Lion's tooth | Blowing wind | Fluffy clock
test: What is the white, fluffy ball of seeds on a dandelion called? | Burr | Taproot | *Clock | Acorn
test: «A long taproot finds water deep down.» What does the taproot do? | Spreads seeds through the air | *Reaches deep down to find water | Closes the flower at night | Holds the flower up in the wind
exam: Why do dandelion seeds need to travel away from their parent plant? | *So they do not compete with the parent for light and water | So bees can count them | So they can sink into the soil at once | So they can glow in the dark
